    "code": "\n# the measurement of the section: a synthetic 1920 x 1080 frame, the median of 50 runs for each version\nimport time\nframe = np.random.default_rng(0).integers(0, 255, (1080, 1920, 3), dtype=np.uint8)\n\ndef pre_no_copy(im):                       # like pre_numpy, without ascontiguousarray\n    x = letterbox(im)[:, :, ::-1].astype(np.float32) / 255.0\n    return x.transpose(2, 0, 1)[None]\n\ndef median_ms(fn, n=50):\n    fn(frame)\n    ts = []\n    for _ in range(n):\n        t0 = time.perf_counter(); fn(frame); ts.append((time.perf_counter() - t0) * 1000)\n    return np.median(ts)\n\nfor name, fn in ((\"letterbox only, 8 bits\", letterbox), (\"letterbox, then NumPy\", pre_numpy),\n                 (\"normalize first, then resize\", pre_float_first), (\"letterbox, then blobFromImage\", pre_blob),\n                 (\"like NumPy, without the copy\", pre_no_copy)):\n    print(\"%-30s %6.2f ms\" % (name, median_ms(fn)))\nprint(\"largest difference, pre_numpy vs pre_blob: %.1e\" % np.abs(pre_numpy(frame) - pre_blob(frame)).max())\n\n# and a Python loop over pixels, the way you would write it in Java, on a 64 x 64 image\nsmall = frame[:64, :64]\ndef loop(im):\n    out = np.empty((3, 64, 64), np.float32)\n    for y in range(64):\n        for x in range(64):\n            for c in range(3):\n                out[2 - c, y, x] = im[y, x, c] / 255.0\n    return out\nt0 = time.perf_counter(); loop(small); tl = time.perf_counter() - t0\nt0 = time.perf_counter()\nfor _ in range(100): np.ascontiguousarray((small[:, :, ::-1].astype(np.float32) / 255.0).transpose(2, 0, 1))\ntv = (time.perf_counter() - t0) / 100\nprint(\"pixel loop on 64 x 64: %.1f ms, vectorized %.3f ms: %.0f times slower\" % (1000 * tl, 1000 * tv, tl / tv))\n",
    "say": "הניסוי של הסעיף: פריים סינתטי של 1920 על 1080, חציון של 50 ריצות לכל גרסה, ההפרש בין שתי הגרסאות הנכונות — ולולאת פייתון על פיקסלים מול הגרסה הווקטורית. בקורס: ‏0.42 / ‏3.49 / ‏8.37 / ‏1.89 / ‏2.35 מ\"ש, והלולאה איטית פי 525. צריך <code class=\"ltr\">pip install numpy opencv-python</code>."
